
= Benchmark Specifications <app-benchmark-specifications>

This appendix records the workload, measurement boundary and available checks for the 26 benchmark cases listed in @benchmark-overview. Case numbers follow the thesis table and differ from the zero-based protocol identifiers. Thesis cases 1–26 map respectively to protocol IDs 0, 1, 12, 13, 14, 2, 5, 6, 8, 16, 17, 7, 18, 19, 27, 4, 10, 20, 21, 25, 3, 11, 15, 22, 23 and 24. Protocol IDs 9, 26 and 28 are intentionally excluded. Timing values are collected in timer ticks unless a different unit is stated. Resources are generally created before a timed operation and released afterwards. Differences mentioned here matter when interpreting measurements across kernels or languages.

Some benchmarks use C measurement or control routines in both the C and Rust configurations. These include interrupt and periodic-timing measurements and selected CPU-load and heap-fragmentation cases. Other benchmarks, such as the payload sweep on Zephyr and NuttX, delegate part of the transfer operation from Rust to C. Consequently, results for these cases include shared C execution and should not be interpreted as comparisons of entirely independent C and Rust workload implementations. These descriptions refer to the firmware revision recorded with the archived measurements; later working-tree revisions are not substituted for that evidence.

+ `timer_overhead` — Two timestamp readings bracket the runner's case-dispatch path. The result includes the timer-access calls and dispatch instructions between them, but not record transmission. Since C and Rust use different dispatch code, this is a measurement-path baseline rather than the isolated cost or resolution of the timer.

+ `queue_roundtrip` — A single task sends a 32-bit value to a capacity-one queue and receives it back. Timing begins before the send and ends after the receive. The returned value must match the sent value. Queue creation and destruction lie outside the timed interval; no other application task is involved.

+ `semaphore_latency` — A single task gives an initially empty binary semaphore and immediately takes it again. Timing covers both operations. As the semaphore is available when taken, no blocking or task hand-off is intended. Successful acquisition is required.

+ `mutex_uncontended` — A single task locks and unlocks a free mutex. Both operations lie between the two timestamps; resource creation does not. No competing task holds the mutex, so priority inheritance and blocking are not exercised.

+ `alloc_latency` — One 64-byte block is allocated and then freed, with both operations included in the timed interval. The returned allocation must succeed. The case uses the kernel-provided allocator where available and is not supported by every Rust-native kernel. Compiler barriers and FFI paths differ between some C and Rust adapters.

+ `task_handoff` — A waiting peer task is released, and timing ends when that peer records its wake timestamp. The interval includes the release operation and the scheduler's transition to the receiver. In the primary FreeRTOS, Zephyr and NuttX ports, the receiver has higher priority and preempts the sender. The Rust-native kernels use different arrangements. The recorded sample does not independently store task priorities or a preemption flag.

+ `yield_roundtrip` — The benchmark task yields to an equal-priority peer and measures the interval until it resumes. The two-task configuration is the baseline. Supported C-kernel ports also use 8, 16 and 32 peers; these are distinct variants, not additional samples of the two-task workload. The implementation checks that a peer ran and may retry the yield up to a fixed limit. The Rust-native kernels use different cooperative release or yielding mechanisms.

+ `preempt_latency` — A higher-priority task waits for a release while a lower-priority task executes. The first timestamp is taken immediately before the release, and the second by the high-priority task after waking. The primary C-kernel implementations release a semaphore waiter; FreeRTOS-in-Rust uses an interrupt-driven release instead. Available sequence checks confirm that the receiver ran.

+ `mutex_priority_inheritance` — A low-priority task holds a mutex, a high-priority task waits to acquire it, and a medium-priority task performs a bounded spin of approximately 2 ms. Timing runs from the high-priority task's lock attempt to successful acquisition. The medium-priority work makes the effect of priority inheritance observable, but there is no automatic pass/fail threshold based on the measured interval.

+ `scheduler_overhead` — The benchmark invokes the scheduler when no alternative application task is ready. Timing covers the invocation and return without an intended context switch. Implementations may reject samples interrupted by a system tick and retry. Ports without a suitable switch-free scheduler path do not provide the case.

+ `notification_latency` — A peer signals the waiting benchmark task. The measured interval starts at the peer's signal and ends when the benchmark task resumes. FreeRTOS uses direct task notification; Zephyr and NuttX use semaphore-based alternatives, and FreeRTOS-in-Rust uses an event-group mechanism. These primitives are functionally similar for this workload but do not have identical implementation costs.

+ `ipc_pingpong` — Two capacity-one queues connect a benchmark task and an echo peer. The benchmark sends a 32-bit request, the peer returns it unchanged, and timing stops when the reply is received. The reply is checked against the request. Queue operations, waiting and task scheduling are part of the timed round trip.

+ `ipc_payload_sweep` — The same request-and-reply pattern is repeated with payload sizes of 1, 4, 16, 64 and 256 bytes. Each size uses its corresponding queues and echo task. Timing includes the transfers and their copying costs, but every returned byte is checked after the end timestamp. Samples are associated with the relevant payload-size variant. Some Zephyr and NuttX Rust paths delegate the transfer to C adapter routines.

+ `ipc_throughput` — A producer sends 16,384 messages of 64 bytes, totalling 1 MiB, through a queue of depth eight. A consumer drains the queue. The measured interval covers the transfer and, on the primary C-kernel paths, consumer completion. The result is converted from the transfer duration into bytes per second. Message counts are checked, but payload bytes are not individually verified. FreeRTOS-in-Rust has a different end condition, so its final queue-drain time may not be included.

+ `zero_copy_ipc` — Two forms of a 256-byte request-and-reply transfer are timed: a copied payload and a reference to shared storage. The reported result is copied time minus reference-transfer time. The returned pointer and payload contents are checked. A non-positive difference is recorded as no observed benefit; its count is retained separately and the sentinel is excluded from positive-saving percentiles. A positive-only distribution must not be interpreted as including all observations.

+ `isr_to_task` — A timer interrupt timestamps handler entry and signals a waiting task. Timing ends when the task resumes after the wait, covering the interrupt-to-task wake-up path. On the paired FreeRTOS, Zephyr and NuttX configurations, the corrected Rust adapter calls the same C measurement routine used by the C runner; the FFI call surrounds rather than interrupts the timed operation. Rust-native kernels have their own timer and signalling arrangements.

+ `trigger_to_isr` — A task records a timestamp, requests a dedicated software-triggered interrupt and compares that timestamp with handler entry. Primary FreeRTOS, Zephyr and NuttX ports mask interrupts before recording the first timestamp, pend the interrupt and then unmask. The Rust-native kernels omit this masking sequence. This measures the implemented software-request-to-entry path, including request and unmasking effects where applicable, rather than a pure hardware exception-entry constant.

+ `nested_irq_overhead` — A lower-priority interrupt handler executes a fixed spin while a higher-priority interrupt is requested ten times. A separate baseline runs without the higher-priority interruptions. The result is the added execution time compared with that baseline. The disabling control is a separate compile-time or manual check rather than a control automatically recorded in every campaign run. Some ports select a minimum from repeated pairs.

+ `tick_overhead` — Available trace hooks capture timestamps around the kernel's periodic tick-processing path. Each sample reports the time attributed to one tick handler, without a deliberately included later application task switch. Hook locations are port-dependent, and kernels without suitable hooks do not report this case.

+ `max_interrupt_disabled` — A timer is configured to interrupt after approximately 50 µs at a priority subject to kernel masking. The reported interval is the observed handler-entry time minus the nominal probe delay. It includes the direct timer-arming sequence present in the port's measurement definition. It is an interrupt-deferral observation, not the full length of a critical section and not a guaranteed maximum. On corrected paired FreeRTOS, Zephyr and NuttX paths, both languages use the same C measurement routine. Extended masking was checked separately rather than within each ordinary run.

+ `periodic_jitter` — The task wakes according to a nominal 1 ms periodic delay. The measured wake-to-wake interval is compared with that nominal period. Because the metric uses consecutive wake intervals, a constant release delay cancels; the result instead captures changes in timing and any persistent period offset. Large deviations are retained rather than automatically treated as missed deadlines.

+ `periodic_jitter_loaded` — A hardware-timer release schedule has a nominal period of 750 µs. Each valid result is the observed activation time minus its expected position on the release grid, so persistent lateness remains visible. Load generation varies by port. STM32F4 and STM32H723 FreeRTOS use settings 0, 100, 150 and 200 to adjust a high-priority interrupt-masked work interval; the settings are not CPU utilisation values. Other boards and kernels use fixed or different sources of load. Missed or coalesced releases produce a sentinel, which is counted separately and omitted from latency percentiles. The release index is realigned after a miss. On corrected paired C-kernel paths, the C and Rust runners call the same C timing function.

+ `sleep_accuracy` — A task requests an approximately 1 ms delay and measures the elapsed time until it resumes. The result is the absolute difference between the measured duration and the nominal request. FreeRTOS uses a one-tick delay, while Zephyr and NuttX use millisecond-based APIs with their respective tick or scheduler semantics. The corrected Zephyr Rust implementation matches the C `k_msleep(1)` conversion and sleep operation; timestamp access may still differ through FFI.

+ `idle_cpu_overhead` — Over a 500 ms idle observation window, trace hooks accumulate the cycles spent handling periodic kernel ticks. The result is this total divided by the window duration, multiplied by one million and reported in ppm. Other kernel activity is not comprehensively counted, so the metric is tick-related CPU share rather than total RTOS overhead. Availability depends on port-specific accounting support.

+ `cpu_overhead_under_load` — A synthetic worker repeats a 10 ms period with nominal busy fractions of 25, 50, 75 and 100 %. It spins for the configured portion and sleeps for the remainder. Each variant measures non-idle CPU share over a 500 ms window, expressed in ppm. The result includes application work as well as kernel activity. The worker does not adjust its spin duration using feedback from the observed sleep time, so the configured fraction is an offered workload rather than an exact measured utilisation.

+ `heap_fragmentation` — The allocator is filled with 256-byte blocks down to a port-specific remaining-free-space margin. Every other block is freed to create separated gaps. The metric is one million times one minus the ratio of the largest available free block to the total free heap; it is reported in ppm. FreeRTOS uses a fixed 16 KiB margin, Zephyr generally uses one eighth of the heap with a minimum margin, and NuttX uses a 20 % margin. FreeRTOS and Zephyr probe allocations to estimate the largest block, while NuttX reads allocator statistics. These differences prevent the metric from representing a strictly identical heap state on all kernels. The defragmented control was checked separately, not as part of every recorded run.
